nodecode-pr-studio is a rewrite of Murali-lns's Murali-lns/hermes-pr-studio:

  https://github.com/Murali-lns/hermes-pr-studio
  commit b9ba1c6bb567a8aeeff60fb4cfd17abebc0fa95f
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Murali-lns (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the reading and writing the pane does, as six model-facing tools and
a command instead of a window. The inbox keeps the original's three GitHub
searches in their order -- review-requested:@me, author:@me, involves:@me,
open only, most recently updated first -- and its rule that a pull request
appears in the first group that holds it, with an optional repository scope.
One pull request is read through the same fields the pane's query asks
for: size, branches, head, draft, review decision, mergeable state, labels,
checks, description, commits, comments and reviews merged into one timeline.
The diff keeps the pane's per-file churn summary and its character budget,
and its thread list keeps the path, line, side, resolved and outdated flags
and the comment ids, which is what a reply needs. The three writes are the
pane's: an issue comment, a reply inside a review thread, and a review of
COMMENT, APPROVE or REQUEST_CHANGES carrying comments on lines of the diff,
with the same refusals (an empty body, a path that climbs out of the
repository, a line below 1, a side that is not LEFT or RIGHT, request changes
with nothing to say). Every call is the user's own gh CLI with an argument
list and no shell, exactly as the Python backend ran it: gh search prs, gh pr
view, gh pr diff and gh api, one timeout each. No token is read or stored,
and gh authenticates itself. Two things are new because a model, not a pane,
is reading: a read_only switch that refuses every write, and one text budget
per answer, so a long patch is cut with a note saying where. The
repository's LICENSE is unchanged beside this file.

Left out: everything that is drawing rather than computing -- the tile and
the status-bar chip, the split and unified diff views, syntax colours, the
line-number gutter and click-to-comment, the image viewer and the proxying of
private-repo images, the persisted open view, the draft queue and the review
bar, and the model picker for "review in a new chat". Also left out: the
per-60-second watcher and its notifications (the model calls the inbox or
reads a PR when asked instead), the HTML allowlist sanitiser and the
GitHub-rendered bodies it cleaned (a model reads markdown, so the plain body
is what these tools answer), the repository dropdown built from gh repo list,
and the "Send to Hermes" prompts, which a cell does not need: the model is
already in the chat the review is being written in.
